<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Cat Says 'Meow'. – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1em; color: #333;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Cat Says 'Meow'.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow very simple classroom instructions with visual cues.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple classroom instructions with visual cues to recognize familiar sounds (e.g., 'Point to the animal that makes the sound.').<br>Can respond to simple questions with sound effects or gestures (e.g., 'What sound is this?').<br>Can respond to simple classroom commands during listening activities (e.g., 'Sit down').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and leads a 'Simon Says' game with simple classroom commands like 'Simon says, sit down,' 'Simon says, stand up,' 'Simon says, touch your nose.' This activates listening skills and prepares students to follow instructions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents large, colorful flashcards of a cat and a dog. The teacher points to the cat and makes a "meow" sound, saying clearly, "This is a cat. The cat says meow." The teacher then points to the dog and makes a "woof" sound, saying, "This is a dog. The dog says woof." The teacher exaggerates pronunciation of /m/ in 'meow' and points to the mouth. The teacher introduces the phonemic awareness of 'C' for cat and 'D' for dog by showing the letter on the flashcard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the cat flashcard: "What sound is this?" (Encourage 'meow' or gesture).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the dog flashcard: "Is this a bird?" (Encourage 'no' or head shake). "What is it?" (Encourage 'dog' or 'woof').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of various animal sounds (cat, dog, rooster, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig), paired with corresponding animal flashcards or projected images. The input format is an auditory 'sound quiz' with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying and describing different animals, based on their sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays an animal sound (e.g., 'meow'). Students listen and point to the correct animal flashcard (e.g., 'cat'). The teacher says, "Listen! What sound is this? Point to the animal."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sound Match:</b> The teacher shows two flashcards (e.g., a rooster and a bird). The teacher plays one sound (e.g., a rooster's crow). Students listen and raise their hand when they hear the sound of the rooster. The teacher uses exclamations: "That's a rooster!" and reinforces /r/ sound.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen for Actions:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of animals with associated actions (e.g., a bird flying, a fish swimming). The teacher says, "The bird can fly," or "The fish can swim." Students listen and point to the correct animal-action pair.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 10-12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Recognizing sounds and associating them with animals; understanding simple WH-questions ('What sound is this?'); using present simple for animal sounds ('The cat says meow.'); practicing /m/ and /r/ pronunciation; introducing vocabulary: cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig; verbs: say, jump, run, fly, swim; adjectives: big, small, loud, quiet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice - "Listen and Circle":</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of 4-5 animals (e.g., cat, dog, duck, cow). The teacher plays a sound (e.g., 'quack'). Students listen and circle the picture of the animal that makes the sound. The teacher repeats the sound and says, "The duck says quack."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice - "Sound Actions":</b> The teacher says an animal name and an action (e.g., "Bird, fly!"). Students listen and perform the action or make the animal sound (e.g., flap arms for 'fly,' 'meow' for 'cat'). The teacher also describes animals using adjectives: "Listen! A big cow says 'moo'." "Listen! A small bird sings quietly."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction - "Guess the Sound":</b> Students work in small groups of two or three. Each student has a set of animal flashcards. One student secretly chooses an animal and makes its sound (e.g., 'bark'). The other student(s) listen and point to the correct flashcard, saying, "Dog!" or "The dog says bark!" The teacher circulates, facilitating and providing sentence frames: "What sound is this?" and "It's a ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 12-15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "What's that Sound?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Animal Sound Orchestra" - A whole-class interactive game where students listen and contribute to a 'sound story'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher holds up an animal flashcard (e.g., 'cat') and makes its sound. Students listen and then imitate the sound chorally: "Meow, meow!" The teacher then says, "The cat says meow. The cat is small." The teacher then introduces a new animal and its sound and description (e.g., 'cow', 'moo', 'big'). As the teacher points to each animal, students listen to the instruction ("Make the sound of the cat!") and respond with the appropriate sound. The teacher also asks, "What sound is this animal making?" for individual students to respond. The activity progresses with students listening to the teacher's cues and responding with the correct animal sound, building a simple 'farm' soundscape. The teacher might introduce 'P' for pig and 'Q' for quack (duck) briefly.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist of students' ability to follow instructions and identify sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Show Me" - The teacher plays a series of distinct animal sounds or says animal names. Students listen and hold up the corresponding animal flashcard from a small set (e.g., cat, dog, duck). The teacher also gives simple commands: "Show me the loud animal!" or "Show me the animal that says 'quack'."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in pointing to the correct animal when hearing its sound.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow simple classroom commands (e.g., 'Show me', 'Point to').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement and participation in listening activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a flashcard of a happy face and a sad face. "Did you like hearing the animal sounds today?" (Students point to happy/sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me the animal that says 'meow'?" (Students point to cat flashcard).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite animal sound today?" (Students can make the sound or point to the animal).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you listen and find the rooster?" (Teacher hides a rooster flashcard, students listen for a crow sound).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified common animal sounds and followed simple commands like 'point to' and 'show me'. The TPR activities and use of actual animal sounds were highly engaging for Kinder learners. Some students still hesitated with making the sounds themselves, requiring more modeling. For next time, I need to ensure more varied opportunities for individual students to respond verbally with single words or sounds. I will also incorporate more movement-based listening activities. I should reinforce the adjectives 'loud' and 'quiet' more explicitly with the sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core animal vocabulary and focused on listening for sounds. The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use the same animal vocabulary in a simple illustrated story or short text, where students will listen and then begin to recognize the written forms of these animal names.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date]</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Time: 2-3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>